.TH GXWI-NETWORK-MANAGER 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-network-manager \- see and change this machine's networks, profiles, name resolution and firewall on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-network-manager
.SH DESCRIPTION
Network Manager is the desktop's window on this machine's networking and
its firewall. Open it from the launcher (type network). It takes no
arguments. Down the side are three groups.
.PP
Network: Overview maps each interface to the network it is on and the
profile it uses, with its addresses, routes, traffic and DHCP lease, and
Renew lease and Reapply. Networks names each network the machine has stood
on and sets its trust level, Private or Public. Profiles and Profile rules
edit the profiles and which interface uses which. DNS looks a name up and
shows the way the question went, flushes resolvd's cache, and sets fallback
servers, search domains and static names.
.PP
Firewall: Exposure shows which services can be reached from private
networks, public networks and this machine, and changes a cell in one step.
Rules writes the firewall's rules, and Test a connection shows what the
firewall would do with one. Activity shows what the firewall has been
deciding.
.PP
System: Port reservations says who may listen on a port. Auditing sets the
lowest level of audit event recorded. Advanced shows the machine's DHCP
identity, and who may look at and change netd's and resolvd's settings.
Permissions..., beside a reservation or beside netd's or resolvd's access,
opens the permissions editor, which shows it read-only to anyone who may
not change it.
.PP
What it shows comes from netd, resolvd, the kernel and the registry under
Machine\eSystem\eNetwork. What it changes it writes to the registry, as the
person, after checking it as the machine would; what the machine would
refuse is not written, and the status line says why. A change that would
cut a desktop connection that is open now asks first. Saving a rule,
changing a cell on Exposure, and other changes that could cut the person
off show a Keep this change? bar, and are undone after 30 seconds unless
kept. The countdown runs on the machine, not in the browser.
.PP
Some of the firewall's live state is example data. Which services are
listening, the connections open now, the firewall's counters and its recent
audit events can't yet be read by programs: only the kernel can read them.
Until they can, Network Manager makes up an example from this machine and
judges it by the real rules. Every section that shows it says Example data,
and the status line says Example. The rules, and everything outside the
firewall, are real.
.PP
What a person may change is what the registry lets them write, key by key,
asked of the machine. Where they may not, a section is shown read-only and
says once that changing it needs an administrator. Exposure and Activity are
for administrators only. Renew lease and Reapply need netd's Network Control
right, which Administrators hold. It reads the machine again every two
seconds, and its settings whenever the registry changes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-network-manager.toml
Its application declaration.
.TP
.I /run/netd/control.sock
netd's control socket: its status, renewals and reapplying.
.TP
.I /run/resolvd/resolv.sock
resolvd's socket: its status, lookups and flushing its cache.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/gxwi-sd-editor
The permissions editor, which Permissions... opens.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR net (1),
.BR resolv (1),
.BR gxwi-sd-editor (1).
.PP
Network Manager, at learn.peios.org.
